// BT-3744 (ADR 0131 Phase 0a): the REPL column of the ADR 0131 probe matrix.
//
// The REPL threads outer locals through its bindings map (the `MethodBody
// (REPL)` row of ADR 0131 §2). Every probe shape here writes the REPL local
// `t` inside a threading construct. The ADR's §"REPL and error examples"
// transcript is the approved display; it comes first, verbatim, followed by
// the o- and s-shapes of the ADR's two probe tables and the §1a sequencing
// shape. Shapes that are not REPL-expressible (o5/o12/o13 write a field, o6/o10
// return with `^` from a method) are covered by the BUnit matrix in
// stdlib/test/adr0131local_rebind_test.bt.
//
// An expression whose answer differs from the ADR today pins today's answer
// under a `PIN-BUG` marker that cites the open issue and names the correct
// answer. Each phase of the BT-3743 epic flipped the pins it fixed; the last
// ones were the REPL loop and fold bodies (frames in BT-3912), `match:` arms
// as producers (BT-3947) and the Phase 0 position diagnostic (retired in
// BT-3913, whose oracle below pins every probe row). Each shape resets `t`
// first, so one shape's wrong answer does not change the next shape's input
// (the ADR transcript is the exception: it is one stateful sequence, as the
// ADR writes it).
//
// Since BT-3745 (ADR 0131 Phase 0b) every shape that crashed or answered
// wrong is a compile error (the position-check error, or §6), so its pin
// asserts that error; a rejected input runs nothing, so the `t` that follows
// it still reads the value before it.

// ===========================================================================
// ADR 0131 §"REPL and error examples" (approved display)
// ===========================================================================

t := 0
// => 0

// o1: the `on:do:` operand leaked its `{Value, StateAcc}` tuple until BT-3745
// (ADR 0131 Phase 0), was a compile error until ADR 0131 Phase 3b (BT-3817),
// when `on:do:`/`ensure:` became local-threading producers.
r := ([t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) + 1
// => 2

t
// => 1

// o4: `collect:` in receiver position raised before BT-3745 and was a compile
// error until BT-3795 (ADR 0131 Phase 2d): the REPL root threads the loop's
// bindings map, so it answers 2 and keeps its write.
(#(1, 2) collect: [:x | t := t + x]) size
// => 2

// The ADR transcript's 4: `t` is 1 after o1.
t
// => 4

// ADR 0131 Phase 4 (BT-3750): the Tier 2 block reaches
// `beamtalk_result:'tryDo:'/2`, a local-threading producer.
(Result tryDo: [t := t + 1. 1 / 0]) isError
// => true

// The protected region raised, so its write is discarded (ADR 0131 §"REPL and
// error examples"): `t` stays 4.
t
// => 4

// ===========================================================================
// o-shapes (operand position)
// ===========================================================================

// o2: `ensure:` as a binary operand.
t := 0
// => 0

// The cleanup block could not see `t` until BT-3745, a compile error until
// ADR 0131 Phase 3b (BT-3817).
r := ([t := t + 1. 1] ensure: [t := t + 1]) + 1
// => 2

t
// => 2

// o3: a read+write conditional as a binary operand.
t := 0
// => 0

// Codegen referenced an unbound `State` (ADR 0131 §3) until BT-3745, a
// compile error until ADR 0131 Phase 3 (BT-3738), when the REPL root made the
// conditional a producer that adopts the bindings map.
r := (4 =:= 5 ifTrue: [2] ifFalse: [t := t + 1. 1]) + 1
// => 2

t
// => 1

// o7: an `on:do:` nested in a conditional arm inside another `on:do:`.
t := 0
// => 0

// The inner tuple leaked, raised, and the outer handler answered 3 (a silent
// wrong answer) until BT-3745, and it was a compile error naming the
// conditional until BT-3823 made the REPL's arms producer frames: the
// protected body and the conditional arm are inlined over the bindings map,
// and the inner `on:do:` at an operand site of the arm adopts it
// (`repl_arm_producer_sites`), as in the compiled contexts (`o7*` in
// adr0131local_rebind_test.bt).
r := [false ifTrue: [2] ifFalse: [([t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) + 1]. 7] on: Error do: [:x | 3]
// => 7

t
// => 1

// o8: as o7 without the trailing `7`.
t := 0
// => 0

// A silent wrong answer (3) before BT-3745, a compile error until BT-3823
// (see o7).
r := [false ifTrue: [2] ifFalse: [([t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) + 1]] on: Error do: [:x | 3]
// => 2

t
// => 1

// o11: the statement-position control. A conditional, then `ensure:`.
t := 0
// => 0

4 =:= 5 ifFalse: [t := t + 1]
// => 1

// Unlike the compiled contexts (where o11 passed), an `ensure:` whose body
// writes a REPL binding could not see it even as a statement
// (`Undefined variable: t`) until ADR 0131 Phase 3b (BT-3817): the construct
// now continues the bindings map, and answers its body's value.
[t := t + 1] ensure: [nil]
// => 2

t
// => 2

// ===========================================================================
// ADR 0131 §1a: a local read before a sibling rebinds it
// ===========================================================================

t := 0
// => 0

// The right operand leaked its tuple until BT-3745, a compile error until ADR
// 0131 Phase 3b (BT-3817). Source order reads `t` as 0 before the right
// operand writes it: 1.
r := t + ([t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0])
// => 1

t
// => 1

// ===========================================================================
// ADR 0131 Phase 3b (BT-3817): `on:do:`/`ensure:` as local-threading producers
// ===========================================================================
//
// The REPL root threads its bindings map, which the construct continues: its
// element 2 is the next bindings map (`lower_repl_construct_tuple`). Each of
// these was a compile error or answered a leaked tuple or `Undefined variable`
// before this phase.

// An `on:do:` as an assignment's whole value.
t := 0
// => 0

r := [t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0]
// => 1

t
// => 1

// A raise in the protected body runs the handler, and the body's write is
// discarded (ADR 0131 §"REPL and error examples").
t := 0
// => 0

r := [t := t + 1. 1 / 0] on: Error do: [:e | t := t + 10. 5]
// => 5

t
// => 10

// An `ensure:` whose cleanup reads the protected body's write.
t := 0
// => 0

r := [t := t + 1. 7] ensure: [t := t + 10]
// => 7

t
// => 11

// An `on:do:` as a statement.
t := 0
// => 0

[t := t + 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0]
// => 1

t
// => 1

// An `on:do:` as a binary operand.
t := 0
// => 0

r := ([t := t + 2. 3] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) * 2
// => 6

t
// => 2

// ===========================================================================
// s-shapes (assign-RHS and statement position)
// ===========================================================================

// s1: `at:ifAbsent:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

// PIN-BUG BT-3913: the Tier 2 block reached the native `at:ifAbsent:`;
// since BT-3745 it is an ADR 0131 §6 compile error. BT-3798 (ADR 0131 Phase
// 2d) made a `Dictionary` lookup a producer in class, value-type and actor
// methods; the REPL keeps the error (its bindings-map lowering has no lookup
// row). Correct answer: 0.
r := #{#a => 1} at: #k ifAbsent: [t := t + 1. 0]
// => ERROR: `t` is assigned in a block passed to `at:ifAbsent:`

// PIN-BUG BT-3913: correct answer 1.
t
// => 0

// s2: `detect:ifNone:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

r := #(1, 2) detect: [:x | x > 5] ifNone: [t := t + 1. 0]
// => 0

t
// => 1

// s3: `inject:into:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

r := #(1, 2) inject: 0 into: [:a :x | t := t + 1. a + x]
// => 3

t
// => 2

// s4: a stored closure, invoked directly.
t := 0
// => 0

// ADR 0133 (BT-3902): storing a block that assigns a captured binding is the
// escaping-block compile error, so the input is refused and `t` is unchanged
// (it used to be stored, and its later `value` raised at run time).
b := [t := t + 1]
// => ERROR: `t` is assigned in a block stored in `b`

t
// => 0

// s5: `ifTrue:ifFalse:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 =:= 5 ifTrue: [2] ifFalse: [t := t + 1. 1]
// => 1

t
// => 1

// s6: an Erlang FFI argument: a block that assigns a captured local is a
// compile error (ADR 0133, BT-3917); a block that only reads `t` is fine and
// `t` stays 0.
t := 0
// => 0

r := (Erlang lists) map: [:x | x + t] with: #(1)
// => [1]

t
// => 0

// s7: `ifNil:` as an assignment RHS.
t := 0
// => 0

// The Tier 2 block was called with the wrong arity until BT-3745, a compile
// error until ADR 0131 Phase 3 (BT-3738).
r := nil ifNil: [t := t + 1. 1]
// => 1

t
// => 1

// s8: a statement-position `do:`.
t := 0
// => 0

#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x]
// => nil

t
// => 3

// s9: `reject:` in receiver position.
t := 0
// => 0

// Raised `invalid argument` before BT-3745 and was a compile error until
// BT-3795 (ADR 0131 Phase 2d), when the REPL root made it a producer.
r := (#(1, 2) reject: [:x | t := t + 1. false]) size
// => 2

t
// => 2

// ===========================================================================
// BT-3753: statement-position constructs nested in a block
// ===========================================================================

// A `do:` in a conditional arm.
t := 0
// => 0

// Leaked the bindings map as its value and left `t` at 0, then was an ADR
// 0131 Phase 0 compile error from BT-3753 until BT-3823: the `do:` is the
// statement of a REPL arm, a producer that adopts the bindings map.
4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x]]
// => nil

t
// => 3

// A loop in a `do:` body: raised `invalid argument` before BT-3753, a
// compile error since, accepted with ADR 0133 Phase 3 (BT-3912 lower loop
// bodies as frames; BT-3913 retires the check).
t := 0
// => 0

#(1) do: [:z | [t < 2] whileTrue: [t := t + 1]]
// => _

t
// => 2

// `nil ifNil:` as a statement.
t := 0
// => 0

// Called the block with the wrong arity before BT-3753, a compile error until
// ADR 0131 Phase 3 (BT-3738).
nil ifNil: [t := t + 1]
// => 1

t
// => 1

// A conditional in a conditional arm answers right and is accepted.
t := 0
// => 0

4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [t := t + 1]]
// => _

t
// => 1

// ===========================================================================
// ADR 0131 Phase 3 (BT-3738): conditionals as local-threading producers
// ===========================================================================
//
// A conditional that writes a binding is a producer at an operand site of a
// REPL input (and the nil-testing family is lowered like the boolean one):
// its `{Value, Bindings}` tuple is bound, the bindings map adopted whole and
// the value read from element 1. Each of these was an ADR 0131 Phase 0
// compile error before this phase.

// `and:` as a receiver.
t := 0
// => 0

r := ((4 =:= 4) and: [t := t + 1. true]) printString size
// => 4

t
// => 1

// `or:` as a receiver.
t := 0
// => 0

r := ((4 =:= 5) or: [t := t + 1. false]) printString size
// => 5

t
// => 1

// `ifNotNil:` with a block parameter, as a binary operand.
t := 0
// => 0

r := (3 ifNotNil: [:v | t := t + v. 10]) + 1
// => 11

t
// => 3

// The two-arm nil forms, the nil arm and the non-nil arm.
t := 0
// => 0

r := (nil ifNil: [t := t + 1. 7] ifNotNil: [:v | 8]) + 1
// => 8

t
// => 1

t := 0
// => 0

r := (5 ifNil: [t := t + 1. 7] ifNotNil: [:v | t := t + 2. v]) + 1
// => 6

t
// => 2

// `ifNotNil:ifNil:` (reversed keyword order) as an operand.
t := 0
// => 0

r := (nil ifNotNil: [:v | t := t + 2. v] ifNil: [t := t + 1. 4]) + 1
// => 5

t
// => 1

// Two locals, written by different arms, as an operand.
t := 0
// => 0

u := 0
// => 0

r := ((4 =:= 4) ifTrue: [t := t + 1. u := 5. 2] ifFalse: [u := 6. 3]) + 1
// => 3

t
// => 1

u
// => 5

// A conditional that is an assignment's whole value, the nil family.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 ifNotNil: [:v | t := t + v. v]
// => 4

t
// => 4

// A conditional operand in a multi-statement input.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 0. r := r + ((4 =:= 4) ifTrue: [t := t + 1. 2] ifFalse: [3]). #[r, t]
// => #[2, 1]

// `on:do:` / `ensure:` as a statement of a REPL loop body was a Phase 0
// error until ADR 0133 Phase 3 lowered loop bodies as frames (BT-3912) and
// retired the check (BT-3913): the loop threads the binding and the handler
// or cleanup runs each iteration.
t := 0. #(1, 2, 3) do: [:x | [t := t + x] on: Error do: [:e | 0]]. t
// => 6

t := 0. #(1, 2, 3) do: [:x | [t := t + x] ensure: [t := t + 10]]. t
// => 36

t := 0. 1 to: 3 do: [:i | [Error signal: "x"] on: Error do: [:e | t := t + i]]. t
// => 6

// ===========================================================================
// ADR 0131 Phase 4 (BT-3750): `Result tryDo:` whose block writes a binding is
// a local-threading producer. A raise discards the block's writes; success
// keeps them.
// ===========================================================================

t := 0
// => 0

// As a statement.
Result tryDo: [t := t + 1. 7]
// => Result ok: 7

t
// => 1

// As an assignment's whole value, raising: the write is discarded.
r := Result tryDo: [t := t + 10. 1 / 0]
// => _

r isError
// => true

t
// => 1

// As an operand, succeeding: the write is kept.
(Result tryDo: [t := t + 5. t]) unwrap + 1
// => 7

t
// => 6

// A binding first written inside a raising block stays undefined.
(Result tryDo: [fresh := 1. 1 / 0]) isError
// => true

// A nested loop writing the binding was a Phase 0 error until BT-3823 made the
// `tryDo:` block a REPL arm: the `do:` is a producer there.
t := 0. Result tryDo: [#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x]. t]
// => Result ok: 3

t
// => 3

// ===========================================================================
// BT-3823: the REPL's arms are producer frames
// ===========================================================================
//
// A conditional arm, an `on:do:` protected body, handler or `ensure:` cleanup
// and a `Result tryDo:` block are inlined over the bindings map (their
// `StateAcc` *is* the map). A loop, fold, conditional, exception construct or
// evaluated block at one of an arm statement's sites (`repl_arm_producer_sites`:
// the statement itself, an assignment's value, a send's operands) adopts that
// map, as one at an operand site of the input does. Each of these was a Phase 0
// compile error, a raise, or (in a handler) a silently dropped write.

// A fold as an operand in a handler.
t := 0
// => 0

r := [1 / 0] on: Error do: [:e | (#(1, 2) inject: 0 into: [:a :x | t := t + x. a + x]) + 1]
// => 4

t
// => 3

// An `on:do:` as a statement of a handler (silently dropped before BT-3823).
t := 0
// => 0

r := [1 / 0] on: Error do: [:e | [t := t + 1] on: Error do: [:e2 | 0]. t]
// => 1

// A loop in an `ensure:` cleanup.
t := 0
// => 0

r := [7] ensure: [#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x]]
// => 7

t
// => 3

// A block `value:` in a handler (raised `invalid argument` before BT-3823).
t := 0
// => 0

r := [1 / 0] on: Error do: [:e | [:k | t := t + k] value: 4. t]
// => 4

// An evaluated block in a conditional arm, and as an operand of the input.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [[t := t + 1] value. t * 10] ifFalse: [0]
// => 10

r := ([t := t + 1. 5] value) + 1
// => 6

t
// => 2

// A fold as an assignment's value in an arm: the arm binds the new local too.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [u := #(1, 2) inject: 0 into: [:a :x | t := t + x. a + x]. u + 1] ifFalse: [0]
// => 4

#[t, u]
// => #[3, 3]

// `tryDo:` as an operand in an arm; a raise discards its write.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [(Result tryDo: [t := t + 1. 5]) unwrap + 1] ifFalse: [0]
// => 6

r := 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [(Result tryDo: [t := t + 10. 1 / 0]) isError] ifFalse: [false]
// => true

t
// => 1

// Arms nested three deep; a raise in the protected body discards the writes
// of the arms inside it.
t := 0
// => 0

// (The cleanup does not read `t`: the `+ 1` after the fold's write may raise,
// and a cleanup that read the write after a raise is a compile error, BT-4003.)
r := [4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [[nil ifNil: [(#(1, 2) inject: 0 into: [:a :x | t := t + x. a + x]) + 1]] ensure: [t := 10]] ifFalse: [0]] on: Error do: [:e | 9]
// => 4

t
// => 10

r := [4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [(#(1, 2) collect: [:x | t := t + x. x]) size. 1 / 0] ifFalse: [0]] on: Error do: [:e | 9]
// => 9

t
// => 10

// The other arm kinds: `ifFalse:`, `and:`, `or:` and the parameter arms of
// the nil family, with a producer at an operand site of the arm.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 =:= 5 ifFalse: [([t := t + 1. 1] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) + 6]
// => 7

r := (4 =:= 4) and: [(#(1, 2) inject: 0 into: [:a :x | t := t + x. a + x]) > 2]
// => true

r := (4 =:= 5) or: [([t := t + 10. 5] ensure: [nil]) > 2]
// => true

t
// => 14

t := 0
// => 0

r := 5 ifNotNil: [:v | (#(1, 2) collect: [:x | t := t + x + v. x]) size + v]
// => 7

r := nil ifNil: [0] ifNotNil: [:v | ([t := t + v] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) + 1]
// => 0

r := 3 ifNil: [0] ifNotNil: [:v | ([t := t + v. v] on: Error do: [:e | 0]) + 1]
// => 4

r := 2 ifNotNil: [:v | (Result tryDo: [t := t + v. v]) unwrap * 10] ifNil: [0]
// => 20

t
// => 18

// A conditional statement of a loop body in an arm, and a `do:separatedBy:`
// in a protected body, adopt the same map.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [#(1, 2) do: [:x | 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [t := t + x]]. t] ifFalse: [0]
// => 3

t := 0
// => 0

r := [#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x] separatedBy: [t := t + 10]. t] on: Error do: [:e | 0]
// => 13

// An evaluated block (`[...] value`, `[:k | ...] value: x`) that writes a
// binding is a REPL arm too: inlined over the bindings map, its sites hold
// producers, even when the write sits behind a nested conditional's loop (in a
// handler that raised `invalid argument` before BT-3823). A block sent a
// `value` selector of another arity is applied, not inlined, so it raises the
// arity error and writes nothing, here as at the input root (BT-3881).
t := 0
// => 0

r := [1 / 0] on: Error do: [:e | [:z | 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x]]] value: 1. t]
// => 3

r := [1 / 0] on: Error do: [:e | [4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x]]] value. t]
// => 6

[:z | 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [#(1, 2) do: [:x | t := t + x]]] value: 1
// => nil

t
// => 9

t := 0
// => 0

r := [1 / 0] on: Error do: [:e | [t := t + 1] value: 1. t]
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 0 but was called with 1

r := [7] ensure: [[t := t + 1] value: 1]
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 0 but was called with 1

r := [1 / 0] on: Error do: [:e | [:k | t := t + k] value. t]
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 1 but was called with 0

r := 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [[:k | t := t + k] value] ifFalse: [0]
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 1 but was called with 0

r := [1 / 0] on: Error do: [:e | [#(1, 2) collect: [:x | t := t + x]] value: 1. t]
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 0 but was called with 1

t
// => 0

// BT-3881: at the input root too, a block sent a `value` selector of another
// arity is applied, not inlined: the arity error, the binding unchanged (the
// argument was ignored, or the parameter read the workspace binding of the
// same name, and the write kept).
[t := t + 1] value: 1
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 0 but was called with 1

t
// => 0

pinArg := 100
// => 100

[:pinArg | t := t + pinArg] value
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 1 but was called with 0

t
// => 0

// BT-3884: the analyser reads the same arity rule, so such a send in a value
// position (an operand, a literal element) is no block evaluation either: it
// raises the arity error instead of the Phase 0 error about a write that
// never happens.
y := ([t := t + 1] value: 1) printString
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 0 but was called with 1

y := ([:k | t := t + k] value) printString
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 1 but was called with 0

#[[t := t + 1] value: 1]
// => ERROR: Wrong number of arguments: block expects 0 but was called with 1

t
// => 0

// A block `value:` whose argument is a writing fold is not lowered: the fold is
// an operand of a construct, not a site, so it keeps the position-check error
// in an arm (as in class, value-type and actor methods) rather than dropping
// the block's write.
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [u := [:k | t := t + k. k] value: (#(1, 2) inject: 0 into: [:a :x | t := t + x. a + x]). u] ifFalse: [0]
// => ERROR: `inject:into:` writes outer local `t`

// A loop body is not an arm: an `on:do:` in a `do:` in an arm was a Phase 0
// error until ADR 0133 Phase 3 lowered loop bodies as frames (BT-3912) and
// retired the check (BT-3913).
t := 0
// => 0

r := 4 =:= 4 ifTrue: [#(1, 2) do: [:x | [t := t + x] on: Error do: [:e | 0]]. t] ifFalse: [0]
// => 3

// A REPL loop whose only write sits in a fold behind a nested conditional. Its
// body threads the bindings map (BT-3823 widened what a REPL loop treats as
// carried), and the fold in the arm of a loop body is a statement, accepted
// since BT-3913: codegen compiles it and answers `t` = 1.
t := 0
// => 0

#(1, 2) do: [:k | (k > 1) ifTrue: [#(1) do: [:j | t := t + j]]]
// => _

t
// => 1

// BT-3877: a conditional statement of a REPL loop body that writes a binding
// tripped the debug verifier (`BindFrameMismatch`). Since BT-3823 the
// conditional is a producer of the fold body, bound in that body's frame.
t := 5
// => 5

u := 0
// => 0

#(1, 2) do: [:k | t > 1 ifTrue: [u := 9]]
// => nil

u
// => 9

// The same body with the write as the arm's own assignment of a fold's value
// is accepted and keeps it.
t := 0
// => 0

#(1, 2) do: [:k | (k > 1) ifTrue: [t := (#(1, 5) inject: t into: [:a :j | a + j])]]
// => nil

t
// => 6

// ===========================================================================
// `match:` arms (BT-3817, BT-3823, BT-3909)
// ===========================================================================
//
// A `match:` arm is an expression, not a block, but it is the same frame a
// conditional's block is (BT-3947, closing BT-3875): a `match:` whose arm writes
// a binding is a REPL construct, each arm inlined over the bindings map, so the
// write comes back. (It was a structured error before: BT-3817 in methods, since
// BT-3909 in the REPL.)

t := 0
// => 0

